sigma_total": 7.13, "baseline_margin": -10.32, "analyst_adjustment": -0.18, "key_factors": ["R+9 district with a Republican incumbent seeking reelection", "All ten expert ratings are Safe/Solid R or Likely R", "Fundraising gap: McDowell has $1.4M raised and $423K on hand versus $11K for Jefferson", "Polymarket prices McDowell around 91% to win"], "rationale": "The district's nine-point Republican lean, McDowell's incumbency, and his lopsided fundraising advantage leave Jefferson with little visible path; every forecaster rates the seat Safe or Solid Republican and markets agree, even allowing for a midterm environment that could favor Democrats.", "watch": "A late wave of outside spending on Jefferson's behalf or an unusually strong national Democratic environment could pull the margin into single digits.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Addison McDowell (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 6th District leans Republican by about nine points, and first-term incumbent Addison McDowell is running for reelection after securing the nomination unchallenged. He faces Democrat Cyril Jefferson, who won his party's primary in March. McDowell has raised roughly $1.4 million to Jefferson's $229,000, and Jefferson reported only about $11,000 in cash on hand in the most recent filing, a steep resource gap this late in the race.\n\nNo public polls track the general election, but every major forecaster rates the seat Safe or Solid Republican, with two outlets calling it Likely Republican. Prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-ten chance of an upset. Campaign coverage has centered on the economy, housing costs, and foreign policy, and the candidates have debated those issues locally, but the district's fundamentals make McDowell a clear favorite heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0525, "margin": -10.38}, {"date": "2026-09